          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is anemia, and does it always mean low iron?",
          "answer": "**What anemia is:** Anemia means a lower-than-normal amount of hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. (Source: \"Iron-deficiency anemia,\" general clinical reference.)\n\n**Does it always mean low iron?** No, and the results I found don't settle this fully. The source says iron deficiency is the most common cause of anemia worldwide. That means it is common, not that it is the only cause. The passage doesn't list other causes, so I can't tell you what they are from these results.\n\n**Why iron matters for the question:** The same source says that in an adult who isn't menstruating heavily or pregnant, low iron raises the question of where the iron was lost. Iron usually leaves the body through bleeding. For that reason, a low iron finding is treated as a clue to investigate, not as a diagnosis on its own. It also says that treating the anemia without finding the cause can miss a gastrointestinal cancer.\n\n**What you could discuss with a clinician:** If you have been told you have anemia, or are wondering about it, ask what type it is, what caused it in your case, and what testing is needed to find out.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
